==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*þurbaną||to need, to have to}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{m|ine-pro|*terp-||to satisfy}}. Germanic cognates include Old Frisian {{m|ofs|*thurva}}, Old Saxon {{m|osx|thurvan}}, Middle Dutch {{m|dum|durven}} (Dutch {{m|nl|durven}}), Old Norse {{m|non|þurfa}} (Swedish {{m|sv|tarva}}), Old High German {{m|goh|thurfan}} (German {{m|de|dürfen}}), Gothic {{m|got|𐌸𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌱𐌰𐌽}}. The Indo-European root also led to Ancient Greek {{m|grc|τέρπω}}, Old Church Slavonic {{m|cu|трѣбѣ}}, Russian {{m|ru|требовать}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈðurvɑn/|lang=ang}}

===Verb===
{{ang-verb}}

# to [[need]], to be [[obliged]] (to do something)

